          ":question/problem": "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=-1$ is{{field-1}}.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (6x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={\\left(\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}\\right)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1)+2}{2\\sqrt{3(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
