          ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}$ at the point where $x=-2.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+24.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 6x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting  $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}}\\right)|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{\\sqrt{3(-2)^{2}+24}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
